e).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"ff2e619583792e70c","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"RCA / design decision — 'in-place live-backend restart on every autodeploy tick'","norm_id":"n59bb051ee37c20cb","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A container that starts then fails readiness repeatedly triggers docker start every tick with no operator-facing off-switch, and there is no described way to revert the new autodeploy behavior without a code change."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Specify MCP backoff ownership and concurrency (are ticks serialized? is the record/clear atomic?) and confirm docker start is idempotent when a container is already being started.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T12","T6","T9","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["T9"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'emit AUTODEPLOY_MCP_SELF_HEAL on success and clear MCP backoff. On failed start/readiness it ... records MCP backoff, and retries on a later tick.'","The backoff read/record/clear is a check-then-act over shared state; the plan does not state whether ticks are serialized or whether concurrent ticks could both issue docker start / race the backoff record."],"finding":"The plan mutates MCP backoff state ('record MCP backoff' / 'clear MCP backoff') but does not specify its lifecycle ownership or concurrency: who owns the backoff record across ticks, whether two overlapping ticks can race the record/clear, and whether docker start is idempotent when a prior tick already started the container.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f93c920ef32a0b74b","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"RCA / design decision — 'records MCP backoff, and retries on a later tick' / 'clear MCP backoff'","norm_id":"nb5ab56da397ba76a","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Two autodeploy ticks overlap: both observe the backend down and both issue docker start / both mutate backoff, producing duplicate SELF_HEAL markers or a lost backoff clear."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State whether AUTODEPLOY_MCP_SELF_HEAL is journal-only or operator-visible-as-metric; if the latter, enumerate the observability.sh offset+publish and monitoring_*.tf alarm edits it requires (mirroring the mcp_retire_cap/mcp_mem_abort wiring at observability.sh:1658-1666).","cohort":["G1G2"],"criteria":["G1G2"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.7,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["grep for AUTODEPLOY_MCP_SELF_HEAL in infra/scripts returns no matches — it is a new token created by this ticket","observability.sh:1658-1666 shows every operator-visible mcp marker (mcp_retire_cap, mcp_mem_abort) gets a dedicated *_OFFSET_FILE + journal_marker_delta publish","plan asserts only the AUTODEPLOY_ERROR/deploy_errors path reuses existing wiring, which covers the FAILURE marker but not the new SUCCESS token"],"finding":"The plan introduces a new success journal token AUTODEPLOY_MCP_SELF_HEAL but does not enumerate its cross-directory consumers; unlike the failure path (AUTODEPLOY_ERROR/mcp-live-backend-down, already wired through observability.sh line 1608 + monitoring_autodeploy.tf), a new token that is operator-visible needs its own offset file + journal_marker_delta publish in infra/scripts/observability.sh and an alarm in infra/terraform/monitoring_*.tf (the pattern used for mcp_retire_cap / mcp_mem_abort at observability.sh:1658-1666).","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fe58d21019134b201","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"RCA / design decision (\"emit AUTODEPLOY_MCP_SELF_HEAL on success\") and Testing plan (\"No Terraform apply is required because the alarm path reuses existing AUTODEPLOY_ERROR/deploy_errors wiring\")","norm_id":"n716cae628773b61c","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Operator/dashboard wants to confirm a self-heal actually occurred (AC3 'operator-visible metric/log/alarm') and finds AUTODEPLOY_MCP_SELF_HEAL is emitted to journald but never published as a metric or surfaced in CloudWatch"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Specify how self-heal resolves the live upstream port to the EXITED container's name for `docker start` (e.g. match on `docker ps -a` name suffix / label rather than `docker port`, which is empty for stopped containers).","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'If the upstream port belongs to a managed MCP container that is not running, autodeploy must ... run docker start <live-container>'","autodeploy.sh:494 mcp_port_of uses `docker port \"$1\" 8091/tcp` which does not report a mapping for an EXITED container","autodeploy.sh:500-511 mcp_image_on_port iterates only `mcp_managed` (running) and matches on mcp_port_of"],"finding":"The plan restarts 'the upstream port [that] belongs to a managed MCP container that is not running' but does not specify how the live upstream port is mapped to the exited container's name, which `docker start` requires — the existing port->container helpers (mcp_port_of/mcp_image_on_port) rely on `docker port`, which reports nothing for a stopped/exited container.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f3ebbb67a3715ac3d","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"RCA / design decision — 'run docker start <live-container>'","norm_id":"n503569a1e83ed460","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["nginx include names port 8093; rebar-mcp-…-8093 is Exited(137); self-heal must resolve the exact container name to docker start it"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State that a RUNNING (healthy) live backend is a strict no-op on the new step — no docker start, no marker, no backoff, no unnecessary readiness re-probe — and add a GREEN test asserting the untouched-running-backend path.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'in-place live-backend restart on every autodeploy tick, before source-change deploy decisions'","ABSENCE: the plan never states the running-backend case is a no-op; the RED tests only cover the exited/failed cases"],"finding":"The plan says the live-backend restart runs 'on every autodeploy tick,' guarded only by 'not running,' but does not state that a healthy, RUNNING live backend is a strict no-op (no docker start, no marker, no backoff, no readiness re-probe), so the common healthy path's expected behavior under the new step is left implicit.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f1e10bc53067d3c07","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"RCA / design decision — 'in-place live-backend restart on every autodeploy tick, before source-change deploy decisions'","norm_id":"n30955a1cb0da8460","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Steady state: live backend is running and serving /mcp on every tick"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] In the design record (AC1 / Rejected alternatives), explicitly compare the chosen in-tick `docker start` self-heal against the established healthcheck-driven container-autoheal/watchdog pattern (e.g. docker-autoheal), stating why folding it into the autodeploy tick — reusing the existing readiness contract, mcp-scoped backoff, and AUTODEPLOY_* markers — is preferred over a separate health-driven restarter.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.7,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'Docker `--restart always` is already present but passive and not tied to readiness or operator-visible recovery evidence'","Plan selects 'in-place live-backend restart on every autodeploy tick ... require the same /health, store-readiness, and startup-handshake contract'","Retrieved web prior art: docker-autoheal (willfarrell/autoheal) and multiple watchdog write-ups describe restarting running-but-unhealthy/exited containers on a health check BECAUSE `--restart always/unless-stopped` does not respect health — i.e. the same gap this plan targets","This is a strengthening/affirmation-level gap: the plan already reuses in-repo blue-green machinery and the parent ticket's verified `docker restart` behaviour, so it is not silently reinventing and carries no unverified capability claim"],"finding":"The design record considers `--restart always` but does not engage with the closest external prior art — the established healthcheck-driven container-autoheal/watchdog pattern (e.g. docker-autoheal and equivalent systemd/bash watchdogs) — which is the direct precedent for 'restart a not-running/unhealthy managed backend gated on a readiness check' and exists precisely because Docker's restart policies are passive and ignore health checks, exactly the RCA's stated gap.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f921bcd008ded2f3d","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"RCA / design decision + Rejected alternatives; AC1 ('selected design is recorded with why alternatives were accepted/rejected')","norm_id":"n647333a0114dc5c1","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A reviewer or future maintainer asks why self-heal is folded into the autodeploy tick rather than run as a standard healthcheck-driven autoheal sidecar/watchdog"],"severity":null,"s